            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Sulla</strong><br>\nLucius Cornelius Sulla Felix was born into a patrician family that had lost most of its wealth by the time he was born. Despite his noble lineage, Sulla's early life was not easy due to his family's diminished status. He received the basic education customary for Roman youths of his time, focusing on Greek and Latin literature, oratory, and Roman law. His lack of money and prospects for a political career forced him to look for alternative ways to improve his standing in Roman society.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Sulla's Military Beginnings</strong><br>\nSulla started his military career in the Jugurthine War, serving under the command of Gaius Marius. His skills and bravery brought him recognition, and he quickly climbed the ranks. During this time, he showcased his abilities both as a tactician and a negotiator, contributing significantly to the capture of Jugurtha, the King of Numidia. Sulla's success in this venture earned him the grass crown, the highest Roman military honor.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Rise to Power</strong><br>\nAfter the Jugurthine War, Sulla's political career advanced as he was elected quaestor, aedile, and then praetor. His leadership in the war against the Cimbri and the Teutones added to his military prestige. When he became consul in 88 BCE, Rome was facing a crisis in the East against King Mithridates of Pontus, who threatened Roman dominion in Asia Minor. Sulla's election as consul poised him to address this conflict head-on.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
