            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Mithridatic Wars</strong><br>\nAs consul, Sulla was initially granted the command against Mithridates, but due to political machinations, the command was transferred to his rival, Marius. Unwilling to surrender his position, Sulla marched on Rome with his legions, seizing control of the city. This bold move marked the first time in Rome's history that a Roman general had taken the city by force. Sulla then reasserted his command and left to fight Mithridates.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Sulla's Reforms</strong><br>\nUpon Sulla's return to Rome after his victory in the East, he declared himself dictator. He implemented a series of reforms aimed at restoring the power of the Senate and reducing that of the popular assemblies and the tribunes of the plebs. Sulla's changes also revised the legal system and sought to stabilize Rome's constitution. However, these reforms were controversial and led to further conflict.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Proscriptions of Sulla</strong><br>\nOne of Sulla's most infamous acts was the proscription, a list of enemies of the state who were to be executed with their property confiscated. This tool was used to eliminate his opponents and reward his supporters. The proscriptions terrorized the populace, as anyone could be added to the list for personal or political reasons. It was a time of fear and violence in Rome.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
