            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Sulla's Later Years</strong><br>\nAfter holding the dictatorship for several years, Sulla did something unexpected: he voluntarily resigned and retired from public life. This act was unprecedented, as Roman dictators before him had not willingly relinquished absolute power. In his retirement, he focused on writing his memoirs and enjoying leisure activities, attempting to live a normal life after years of warfare and politics.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy of Sulla</strong><br>\nSulla's legacy is complex, marked by his exceptional military successes and the ruthless efficiency of his rule. His career was a precursor to the end of the Roman Republic and the rise of the Roman Empire. His actions demonstrated the vulnerability of Roman political institutions to military power. Though he aimed to strengthen the republic, some of his reforms inadvertently hastened its decline.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Sulla's Death and Aftermath</strong><br>\nSulla died in 78 BCE, leaving behind a reformed state and a controversial reputation. His death did not lead to the restoration of peace; rather, it was followed by more civil strife as different leaders vied for power. The Roman Republic continued to struggle with internal conflicts, which ultimately culminated in the establishment of the Roman Empire under Augustus. Sulla's life and death underscored the turmoil of his times and the transformative period in which he lived.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
